# Do not delete this file.
#
# This file lists jar files and native libraries that are no longer required
# by ArtiSynth, so that they can be removed from lib/ whenever the libraries
# listed in lib/LIBRARIES are installed or verified. Older versions of the
# libraries in LIBRARIES are removed automatically, and do not need to be
# listed here, unless they can't be matched to their replacements (e.g.,
# because the library was renamed).
#
# The format is the same as for LIBRARIES: each name must be on a separate
# line, the character '#' comments out all remaining characters to the end
# of the line, and names may be preceded by an optional system specifier,
# in which case they are removed only on matching systems.
#
# Names may also contain the wildcard character '*', which matches any
# sequence of characters, subject to the following rules:
#
# Jar file names must end in .jar and must not begin with '*'; e.g.,
# 'jogl-all-*-natives-windows-i586.jar'.
#
# Native library names can be generic, in which case they are transformed
# into system-specific names, as in LIBRARIES, and '*' can appear only at the
# end of the version suffix. 'PardisoJNI.2021.1.1' matches only that version,
# 'PardisoJNI.2021.*' matches all 2021 versions, and 'PardisoJNI.*' matches
# all versions, including a file with no version.
#
# Native library names can also be system-specific, such as libgomp.so.1, in
# which case they match only on the indicated system. '*' can appear anywhere
# after at least one character of the base name, e.g., 'libiomp5*.dylib' but
# not 'lib*.dylib'.
#
# Libraries listed in LIBRARIES always take precedence: jar files are never
# removed if their names, with versions removed, match a jar file listed in
# LIBRARIES (for any system), and native libraries listed in LIBRARIES for
# the current system, and their links, are never removed. Names with
# wildcards also never remove other versions of those native libraries,
# while exact names do: e.g., 'gdcmjni.dll' removes that file even though
# gdcmjni.3.0.22.1.dll is in use.
#
